Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Adapted for the Screen by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the novella by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S STUDY - NIGHT

A modest Victorian study lit by fire and candle. Shelves groan under old books. NARRATOR, a bearded man in a dark frock coat, sits at a writing desk facing camera, holding a small volume.

NARRATOR
Marley was dead, to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that.

(SOUND: distant church bells tolling, a low winter wind rattling the window panes)

The register of his burial was signed by the clergyman, the clerk, the undertaker, and the chief mourner. Scrooge signed it.

NARRATOR
(leaning forward)
Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

He closes the book with a soft thud. The fire flares.

(SOUND: sharp crackle of the fire, echoing like a rattling chain)

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - LATE AFTERNOON

A dim, freezing office. Frost patterns the windows. SCROOGE, a hard, cold-eyed man with frosty hair and a thin blue-lipped scowl, hunches over his ledgers. In the adjacent tiny cell, BOB CRATCHIT rubs his hands at a candle, wearing a long white comforter.

(SOUND: muffled wheezing of pedestrians outside, stamping feet on pavement stones)

The door bursts open. FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, glowing nephew, enters briskly.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle? You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug! If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
There are many things from which I might have derived good by which I have not profited. Christmas among the rest. A good time, a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good and will do me good. And I say, God bless it!

In the tank, Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes his fire guiltily.

(SOUND: faint single coal crackling in the tiny fire, wind howling at the keyhole)

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter, books in hand.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe? Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I help to support the prisons and the workhouses. Those who are badly off must go there. If they would rather die, they had better do it and decrease the surplus population.

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge returns to his labours.

(SOUND: city clocks striking three, a distant carol singer beginning "God bless you, merry gentleman")

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - DUSK

The office grows darker. Scrooge rises from his stool with ill will. Bob Cratchit snuffs his candle and dons his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day tomorrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient. And it's not fair. But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises and slides away down the street with boys.

(SOUND: boys' laughter and sliding on ice, fading into fog)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy yard. Fog and frost cling to the black gateway. SCROOGE gropes to his door. The large knocker stares back -- then transforms into MARLEY'S GHOST, spectacles on its forehead, hair stirring.

(SOUND: low ominous rumble like distant thunder, a single chain clink)

Scrooge recoils, turns the key, enters, lights a candle and checks every room. Satisfied, he sits by a meager fire in his dressing-gown, eating gruel. The tiles on the fireplace seem to show Marley's face on every one.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A bell in the room begins to swing, then every bell in the house.

(SOUND: cacophony of ringing bells swelling then stopping abruptly)

A clanking noise rises from below -- chains dragged across casks. The cellar door booms. The noise ascends the stairs and passes through the locked door.

MARLEY'S GHOST enters, transparent, wrapped in a chain of cash-boxes, keys, and ledgers. Its jaw is bound with a kerchief. The fire leaps then dies.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much. Who are you?

SCROOGE
Ask me who I was.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Who were you, then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

The Ghost removes its bandage; its jaw drops to its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellow-men. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link. Mankind was my business! The common welfare was my business! Hear me! My time is nearly gone. You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Expect the first tomorrow when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more!

The Ghost binds its head, floats backward. The window flies open. A chorus of lamenting phantoms wails outside, all chained. Marley's Ghost joins them and vanishes into the night.

(SOUND: mournful wailing and clanking chains fading into silence, wind rushing in)

Scrooge slams the window, checks the locks, and collapses exhausted into bed.

(SOUND: heavy bolt sliding home, distant clock striking twelve)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The clock strikes one. Light flashes. The bed curtains are drawn aside by a hand. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST appears -- a strange childlike old man with white hair, a jet of light from its head, holding holly and summer flowers.

(SOUND: soft ethereal chime, like distant sleigh bells under a gentle wind)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Rise! And walk with me!

It clasps Scrooge's arm. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Snowy fields. Scrooge sees his boyhood self reading alone in a desolate schoolroom.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

The Ghost shows a laughing little girl, FAN, embracing young Scrooge.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Father is so much kinder than he used to be. Home, for good and all!

They vanish. The scene shifts.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (FLASHBACK)

A warm, bright room cleared for dancing. OLD FEZZIWIG laughs jovially. Young Scrooge and DICK WILKINS dance with apprentices, Mrs. Fezziwig, and others to a fiddler's tune.

(SOUND: lively fiddle music swelling, feet stomping in joyful rhythm)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work tonight. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer!

Later, alone with the Ghost, adult Scrooge watches his younger self part from a gentle young woman, BELLE, in mourning.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me. A golden one. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more!

The Ghost forces him to watch Belle now a happy mother with her children and husband.

HUSBAND
Mr. Scrooge sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

Scrooge seizes the Ghost's cap and extinguishes its light. He reels back into his bed.

(SOUND: music and laughter cutting off sharply into silence, a single heavy heartbeat)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes to a prodigious snore. The clock strikes one again. A blaze of ruddy light fills the room. He draws back his own curtains. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a majestic Giant in green robe and holly wreath, sits on a throne of food and greenery, holding a glowing torch.

(SOUND: roaring chimney fire, cheerful crackle of chestnuts popping)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! And know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Touch my robe!

They stand in snowy city streets. People shovel snow with jovial calls. They enter a baker's where the Ghost blesses poor dinners with incense from its torch.

They arrive at Bob Cratchit's humble home. The family bustles. MRS. CRATCHIT and daughters prepare. TINY TIM, small and lame on a crutch, rides on Bob's shoulder.

(SOUND: bubbling potatoes, merry family chatter and laughter)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Later at FRED's bright home, laughter rings as they play Yes and No. Fred's wife and friends roast Scrooge good-naturedly.

FRED
He said Christmas was a humbug. Ha, ha, ha! Uncle Scrooge!

The Ghost reveals two wretched children from its robe -- Ignorance and Want.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy. Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The Ghost ages and vanishes as the bell strikes twelve.

(SOUND: deep bell tolling twelve times, a low ominous drone building underneath)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A hooded, silent PHANTOM, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, glides in, pointing onward. Scrooge trembles.

SCROOGE
I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. Lead on, Spirit!

They stand on 'Change. Businessmen discuss a dead man's cheap funeral with yawns and laughs.

They move to a filthy rag-and-bone shop. A charwoman, laundress, and undertaker's man sell a dead man's belongings to OLD JOE, laughing.

CHARWOMAN
He frightened everyone away when alive, to profit us when dead! Ha, ha!

They arrive at a dark deathbed. A body lies under a sheet. The Phantom points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the veil.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me!

The Phantom reveals the Cratchit home. The family sits quiet. Bob has returned from visiting Tiny Tim's grave.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little child! My little, little child!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

They speak of kindness from Fred. The Phantom leads Scrooge to a neglected grave in a churchyard.

The stone reads: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Phantom's hand trembles. Scrooge clutches it desperately. The figure shrinks, collapsing into a bedpost.

(SOUND: wind rising to a shriek then sudden dead silence, a single distant church bell striking one)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Sunlight streams in. Scrooge leaps from bed, glowing, tears on his face. He laughs wildly, dressing in a frenzy.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather! I am as happy as an angel! A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world! Hallo! Whoop! Hallo!

He flings open the window. Clear, bright day. Bells peal.

(SOUND: glorious pealing church bells, joyous and loud, children's laughter below)

A BOY in Sunday clothes stands below.

SCROOGE
What's today, my fine fellow?

BOY
Why, CHRISTMAS DAY!

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know the Poulterer's in the next street but one? Go and buy the big prize Turkey! Come back with the man and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy races off. Scrooge chuckles, writes an address, pays the poulterer and cab with laughter.

(SOUND: turkey gobble, coins clinking, Scrooge's booming laugh)

EXT. STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

Scrooge strides out in his best clothes, beaming at everyone. He meets one of the PORTLY GENTLEMEN.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! A merry Christmas to you! I hope you succeeded yesterday. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness to accept...

He whispers a large sum. The gentleman is astonished.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. Come and see me.

He continues, patting children, greeting beggars, looking into windows with delight.

(SOUND: merry street bells and carols, footsteps crunching snow with purpose)

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY - EVENING

A warm, laughing gathering. FRED and his wife play games with guests. Scrooge knocks and enters timidly.

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED embraces him heartily. The room erupts in welcome. Scrooge joins the games, laughing.

(SOUND: harp music playing a familiar childhood air, overlapping laughter and cheers)

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - BOXING DAY - MORNING

Scrooge waits at his open door. Bob Cratchit enters eighteen minutes late, hat off, hurrying to his stool.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir.

Bob approaches fearfully. Scrooge leaps up, digs him in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob stares, then smiles. Scrooge laughs.

(SOUND: coal scuttle rattling as it's filled, warm crackling fire building, distant joyful bells)

INT. NARRATOR'S STUDY - NIGHT

The same study. The fire burns brightly. NARRATOR closes his volume, smiling at camera.

NARRATOR
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

He raises a glass.

NARRATOR
And so, as Tiny Tim observed...

TINY TIM (V.O.)
God bless Us, Every One!

(SOUND: final warm swell of bells and a single child's laugh fading to gentle silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END
